module Main
import Std.Bytes

-- FFI Slice 4 (finding #4): cross-tier composition -- an ADOPTED foreign-bytes
-- cell (0xFFFB WokForeignBytes, produced by Libc.strndup's owned-return adopt
-- tier from FFI Slice 2) fed DIRECTLY into the owned-INTO-C `Sink.consume`
-- router (Slice 4). Neither Task-4 fixture (00/01) drives this composition:
-- both start from a freshly-built (fromList) 0xFFFC WokBytes cell, never a
-- 0xFFFB adopted one. This fixture closes that gap.
--
-- `Libc.strndup`'s result is used exactly ONCE (as the sole argument to
-- `Sink.consume`), so Perceus emits no dup before the call -> the runtime
-- refcount is 1 at the dispatch -> the router takes the MOVE branch: it frees
-- the adopted buffer exactly once (0 copy bytes), same discipline as
-- 00-consume-unique.wok but over a 0xFFFB cell instead of a 0xFFFC one. If the
-- MOVE branch mishandled the adopted cell's destructor (e.g. skipped `free`,
-- or freed it via the wrong path), this would show up as a leak (rc stats,
-- Suite B) or a double-free/UAF (ASan, via scripts/asan-runtime.sh).
--
-- Input: strndup [65,66,67,68] 4 => Bytes[65,66,67,68] (A,B,C,D, no NUL, n ==
-- length so no truncation). consume then folds it into the FNV-1a checksum
-- compared differentially across the reference/RC-abstract/RC-CHeap backends.
foreign module Libc "c" free "free" where
  owned strndup : Bytes -> U64 -> Bytes with IO
foreign module Sink "wok" where
  consume "consume" : owned Bytes -> U64 with IO

main : U64
main = Sink.consume (Libc.strndup (fromList [65, 66, 67, 68]) 4)
